B012.after:
For database $\mathcal X=\{x_i\}_{i=0}^{N-1}\subset\mathbb R^d$ with stable IDs, exact top-$k$ selects the smallest distances
\begin{equation}
 \rawdist(q,x)=\|q-x\|_2^2.
 \label{eq:2-raw}
\end{equation}
We use the stored GT top-10 set, including its tie choices. Recall@10 measures how many of those IDs ANN recovers (\cref{eq:3-recall}); it is not a worst-case distance guarantee.

B016.after:
With at least $k$ valid routed candidates, the survivor cap is $c(\alpha)=\max\{k,\lceil\alpha k\rceil\}$. The output and survivor sets satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal R_\alpha(q)\subseteq C_\alpha(q)\subseteq U(q)\subseteq\mathcal X.
 \label{eq:2-cascade}
\end{equation}
Refinement can reorder retained vectors, but cannot recover excluded IDs. Stage-wise GT coverage bounds final recall; measuring each stage's loss needs counterfactual scoring over a fixed universe.

B019.after:
The inherited primary levels are Gaussian mid-quantiles. Under an exact Gaussian model, zero-threshold one-bit MSE-optimal levels would instead be $\pm\sigma\sqrt{2/\pi}\simeq\pm0.79788\sigma$. For fixed signs and positive scale, however,
\begin{equation}
 \primarydist=\|q'\|^2+da^2-2a\langle q',s\rangle,
 \qquad s\in\{-1,+1\}^d.
 \label{eq:2-sign-order}
\end{equation}
Thus scale cancels from primary ordering but still affects residual reconstruction. Our design uses separability and measured quality, without claiming optimal quantisation.

B020.after:
$\rawdist$ uses original vectors; $\primarydist=\|q'-\widehat p\|^2$ uses primary reconstructions. The refined score is
\begin{equation}
 \refineddist=\|q'-\widehat p-\widehat r\|^2+\|q'\|^2.
 \label{eq:2-refined}
\end{equation}
Its query-only term changes distance values, not ranking. Routing and reconstruction errors remain after exact computed-score selection.

