\chapter{Research Design and Measurement}
\label{ch:methodology}
\chaptermark{Research Design and Measurement}
We use paired execution tests, held-out calibration and tuned system frontiers. Each needs different controls. This chapter defines them; Chapters 4--6 describe the methods and Chapter 7 reports results.

B056.after:
The constructor estimates scale as
\begin{equation}
 \sigma^2=\frac{1}{n_t d}\sum_{i=0}^{n_t-1}\|x_i\|_2^2,
 \qquad D_s=d/M,\qquad K=2^B.
 \label{eq:4-scale}
\end{equation}
For $B=D_s=8$, levels are $\{-a,+a\}$ with $a=0.674\sigma$: one byte stores eight signs. JHQ's analytical structure \cite{jhq} allows $O(d)$ encoding and exact small-LUT factorisation (\cref{sec:5-2}). Arbitrary Lloyd-refined codewords need not remain separable.

B057.after:
For fixed signs $\widehat p=a s$, $s_j\in\{-1,+1\}$,
\begin{equation}
 \|q'-a s\|^2=\|q'\|^2+da^2-2a\langle q',s\rangle.
 \label{eq:4-sign-ranking}
\end{equation}
Positive $a$ does not change real-arithmetic primary order, without a Gaussian assumption. It does change residuals and refined ranking. Zero scale is degenerate; FP32 ties still matter.

B066.after:
With one-byte primary subspace codes, resident row and offset bytes are
\begin{equation}
 S_{\rm rows}=N\left(M+\left\lceil\frac{dB_r}{8}\right\rceil+8\right),
 \qquad S_{\rm offsets}=4(n_{\rm list}+1).
 \label{eq:4-row-storage}
\end{equation}
IDs and FP32 corrections add eight bytes per row. At $M=d/8$, $B_r=8$, codes take $9d/8$ bytes, 12.5\% above SQ8. Graph-free storage is not a unique compression advantage over int8.

B074.after:
The one-pass capacity estimate is
\begin{equation}
 H_{\rm single}=N(2b_r+3M+20)+2^{30}\quad\text{bytes}.
 \label{eq:4-peak-heuristic}
\end{equation}
Ten live gather/transpose arrays contribute $3NM+2Nb_r+20N$ bytes: three primary copies, two residual/correction copies, assignments, sort order and IDs. The extra 1\,GiB is heuristic headroom, not a full peak model.

B075.after:
At final two-pass repack, IDs, residuals, corrections and both primary layouts occupy
\begin{equation}
 S_{\rm repack,2}=N(b_r+2M+8).
 \label{eq:4-two-pass-repack}
\end{equation}
This counts that phase only. Whole-build peak also considers earlier pipeline/inverse scratch, model, sort, context and later workspace allocations.

B085.after:
Primary ADC keeps $q'$ in FP32 and reconstructs stored codes \cite{pq,jhq}:
\begin{equation}
 T_m[z]=\|q'_m-\mathcal C_m[z]\|_2^2,\qquad
 \primarydist(q,x)=\sum_{m=0}^{M-1}T_m[z_m(x)].
 \label{eq:5-primary-lut}
\end{equation}
An eight-bit label normally needs 256 entries. The useful extra structure is inside each Cartesian subcodebook, beyond the PQ subspace partition.

B086.after:
At $K=256$, $D_s=8$, the analytical codebook is
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 a&=\sigma\Phi^{-1}(3/4)\simeq0.67449\sigma,\qquad
 \mathcal C_m=\{-a,+a\}^{8},\\
 \primarydist(q,x)&=\|q'\|_2^2+da^2
       -2a\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}s_j(x)q'_j.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:5-sign-codebook}
\end{equation}
$\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF. Each byte stores eight signs, higher bits first. For $a>0$, primary order equals sign-vector inner-product order. Cartesian structure also permits exact four-/eight-way splits; a learned replacement needs compatibility checks.

B087.after:
The high nibble selects the first four coordinates and the low nibble selects the last four: $\mathcal C_m[16h+l]=(A_m[h],B_m[l])$. Their tables are
\begin{equation}
 T_m^{\rm hi}[h]=\|q'_{m,\rm hi}-A_m[h]\|_2^2,\qquad
 T_m^{\rm lo}[l]=\|q'_{m,\rm lo}-B_m[l]\|_2^2.
 \label{eq:5-lut-halves}
\end{equation}
Disjoint squared-L2 sums give
\begin{equation}
 T_m[z]=T_m^{\rm hi}[z\!\gg\!4]
        +T_m^{\rm lo}[z\mathbin{\&}15].
 \label{eq:5-lut-factorisation}
\end{equation}
This stores 32 entries instead of 256, without changing real-arithmetic scores, codes or candidates.

B091.after:
Four subspace bytes from one candidate form one word:
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf{w}[j,p]=\sum_{t=0}^{3}2^{8t}Z[4j+t,p],\qquad
 \operatorname{offset}_{\rm bytes}(\mathsf{w}[j,p])=4(jN+p).
 \label{eq:5-packed-layout}
\end{equation}
Shifts and masks recover each byte exactly: $(\mathsf w[j,p]\gg(8t))\mathbin{\&}255$. Its nibbles index \cref{eq:5-lut-factorisation}. This packs subspaces of one vector, not four candidates.

B102.after:
Nonnegative FP32 squared-sum scores and positions are packed as
\begin{equation}
 \kappa(D,p)=2^{32}\operatorname{uintbits}_{32}(D)+p.
 \label{eq:5-packed-key}
\end{equation}
Unsigned comparison matches lexicographic order for positive-zero, non-NaN scores and valid nonnegative positions. Infinity follows finite scores; the initial all-one word follows valid keys. Invalid positions cannot publish bounds.

B113.after:
With primary reconstruction $\widehat p$, residual $\widehat r$ and stored correction $\gamma=2\langle\widehat p,\widehat r\rangle$, refinement computes
\begin{align}
 \refineddist(q,x)
 &=\primarydist(q,x)+\|q'-\widehat r(x)\|_2^2+\gamma(x)
 \label{eq:5-refined-score}\\
 &=\|q'-\widehat p(x)-\widehat r(x)\|_2^2+\|q'\|_2^2.
 \label{eq:5-refined-constant}
\end{align}
Norm expansion gives the second equality. The query-only constant preserves ranking, while reconstruction error remains \cite{jhq}.

B122.after:
Signed zeros first map to positive zero; then float bits $u$ map to
\begin{equation}
 F(u)=\begin{cases}
       \mathop{\sim}u,&\text{if the sign bit is set},\\
       u\mathbin{|}2^{31},&\text{otherwise}.
      \end{cases}
 \label{eq:5-probe-key}
\end{equation}
This orders non-NaN values, including negatives and infinities, unlike \cref{eq:5-packed-key}. Equal zeros share a key. Every pass uses the same rounded \texttt{fma}, keeping recomputed keys fixed.

B128.after:
For routed universe $U(q)$, JHQ's refinement multiplier sets \cite{jhq}
\begin{equation}
 c(\alpha)=\max\{k,\lceil\alpha k\rceil\},\qquad
 C_\alpha(q)=\operatorname{Top}^{\rm primary}_{\min\{c(\alpha),|U(q)|\}}U(q).
 \label{eq:6-budget}
\end{equation}
$C_\alpha$ contains valid survivors; underfull output slots receive sentinels. Analysis requires at least $k$ valid candidates. $\alpha$ changes retention within $U$, while probes change $U$ itself.

B130.after:
For fixed batch and routing, the cost split is
\begin{equation}
 T(\alpha)=T_{\rm base}+T_{\rm select}(c(\alpha))+
 T_{\rm refine}(c(\alpha))+T_{\rm final}(c(\alpha)).
 \label{eq:6-cost-model}
\end{equation}
$T_{\rm base}$ covers transfers, rotation, routing and primary scores; survivor-dependent work follows \cref{fig:6-budget-scope}. Capacity/resource thresholds prevent a monotone-latency claim. The rule minimises accepted budget, not noisy QPS (\cref{sec:7-budget-trace}).

B135.after:
\paragraph{A finite-pool extension.}
Assume each query has fixed $X_q(\alpha)$ at the intended execution regime, independent of subset membership/position. For pool size $N_{\rm cal}$ and counts $n_j=|\{q:X_q=j\}|$, uniform sampling without replacement accepts only all-zero samples or one single-count query \cite{nist_hypergeometric}:
\begin{equation}
 \Pr\{D_S(\alpha)\leq1\}
 =\frac{\binom{n_0}{S}+n_1\binom{n_0}{S-1}}{\binom{N_{\rm cal}}{S}}.
 \label{eq:6-finite-pool}
\end{equation}
Out-of-range binomial coefficients are zero. Predicting GT risk also needs the grid acceptance structure and held-out fixed-arm quality. Recorded subset sums test count invariance (\cref{sec:7-budget-trace}).

B142.after:
\paragraph{A rank-based extension.}
With the same nesting/fixed-score conditions and one-based primary rank,
\begin{equation}
 D_S(\alpha)=\sum_{q\in\mathcal Q_S}\sum_{x\in\mathcal R_{\rm ref}(q)}
 \mathbf1\{\operatorname{rank}_{\rm P}(x)>c(\alpha)\}.
 \label{eq:6-rank-curve}
\end{equation}
Exported reference ranks could recover the whole curve from one search. The current harness instead reruns every budget; rank extraction remains future work.

B143.after:
Let $\widehat r,\widehat Q$ denote held-out recall/QPS and $\widehat r^*=\max_{\alpha\in A}\widehat r(\alpha)$. The diagnostic oracle is
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_{\rm oracle}\in\operatorname*{arg\,max}_{\alpha\in A}
 \left\{\widehat Q(\alpha):
 \widehat r(\alpha)\geq\widehat r^*-\delta\right\},\qquad\delta=10^{-3}.
 \label{eq:6-oracle}
\end{equation}
It uses labels and timings. Its recall slack $\delta$ differs from agreement's slot allowance $\epsilon$.

